{"embedding_dim": 1536, "data": [{"__id__": "chunk-c16922036754e01bfff65655734f9cfc", "__created_at__": 1756582225, "content": "{\"feature\": \"Trial run of video replies in EU/EEA\", \"feature_description\": \"Roll out video reply functionality to users in EEA only. GH (Geo-handler; a module responsible for routing features based on user region) (Geo-handler) will gate exposure by region; BB (Baseline Behavior; standard user behavior used for anomaly detection) (Baseline Behavior) will collect baseline telemetry/analytics for anomaly detection and feedback.\", \"needs_geo_specific_logic\": true, \"reasoning\": \"Multiple jury reports converge: the feature is region-targeted (EEA/EU) and implements user-generated video content and analytics, which triggers EU-specific obligations. The Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065) applies to hosting service providers and imposes notice-and-action, transparency, user redress, recommender-system and (if designated) VLOP risk/audit obligations (Articles 14–16, 15/24, 20, 27, 33–37). Data-protection rules under the GDPR apply to BB (Baseline Behavior; standard user behavior used for anomaly detection) telemetry and any personal data processing (Art. 4(5) pseudonymisation; Art. 5(1)(c),(e) minimisation/storage limitation; Art. 6(1)(a)/(f) lawful bases; Art. 8 child-consent; Art. 46 SCCs for transfers). Practical product implications requiring geo-specific logic: (1) GH (Geo-handler; a module responsible for routing features based on user region) must enforce EEA-only exposure at routing/delivery; (2) implement notice-and-action intake, triage, takedown, logging and redress workflows to support DSA reporting; (3) instrument telemetry/BB (Baseline Behavior; standard user behavior used for anomaly detection) to minimise personal data, pseudonymise where required, and permit opt-outs; (4) ensure retention and cross-border transfer controls (SCCs/art.46) and GDPR lawful-basis management (consent vs legitimate interest, age checks per Art.8); (5) prepare for VLOP-level obligations (systemic risk assessments, audits) if thresholds are met. Traceability note: source document chunks provided do not contain full CELEX article text for each cited DSA article—legal teams should validate precise obligations and reporting formats against the EUR-Lex authoritative text. Also confirm whether rollout scope is EEA (feature text) or EU (some metadata used); GH (Geo-handler; a module responsible for routing features based on user region) configs should reflect the confirmed scope.\", \"regions_affected\": {\"region\": \"EEA (confirm whether EU or EEA; feature text states EEA)\", \"requirement_summary\": \"Feature is user-generated video content delivered into the EEA and uses behavioral telemetry. Implement EEA-only exposure gating, DSA notice-and-action + transparency + redress controls, algorithmic/recommender documentation if replies are surfaced by algorithms, GDPR-compliant telemetry (minimisation, pseudonymisation, lawful basis, age-consent), and SCCs/transfer safeguards for any third-country storage/processing.\", \"regulations\": [{\"name\": \"Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)\", \"citation\": \"Articles 14–16\", \"snippet\": \"Obligations for hosting service providers to operate notice-and-action mechanisms and take appropriate and expeditious action upon receiving notice of illegal content.\", \"source_id\": \"[DC] digital_services_act_wiki.txt\"}, {\"name\": \"Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)\", \"citation\": \"Article 15 / Article 24 (transparency reporting)\", \"snippet\": \"Transparency reporting obligations for providers of intermediary services (publishable, machine-readable reporting at least annually) including content-moderation decisions, complaints handling, and use of automated tools.\", \"source_id\": \"EUR-Lex 32022R2065\"}, {\"name\": \"Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)\", \"citation\": \"Article 20\", \"snippet\": \"Internal complaint-handling system for users to challenge content moderation decisions and receive reasoned responses.\", \"source_id\": \"EUR-Lex 32022R2065\"}, {\"name\": \"Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)\", \"citation\": \"Article 27\", \"snippet\": \"Transparency obligations for recommender systems and algorithms that determine content ranking/surfacing; documentation for algorithmic decision-making that materially affects users.\", \"source_id\": \"EUR-Lex 32022R2065\"}, {\"name\": \"Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)\", \"citation\": \"Articles 33–37\", \"snippet\": \"Designation threshold and VLOP obligations (>45M MAU), annual systemic risk assessments (Article 34), and independent audits/audit implementation reports for VLOPs (Article 37).\", \"source_id\": \"EUR-Lex 32022R2065\"}, {\"name\": \"General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR)\", \"citation\": \"Art. 4(5), Art. 5(1)(c), Art. 5(1)(e), Art. 6(1)(a)/(f), Art. 8, Art. 46(2)(c)\", \"snippet\": \"‘Pseudonymisation’ definition [Art.4(5)]. Personal data must be adequate, relevant and limited (data minimisation) [Art.5(1)(c)] and kept no longer than necessary (storage limitation) [Art.5(1)(e)]. Processing lawful if subject has given consent [Art.6(1)(a)] or on other lawful bases (e.g., legitimate interests [Art", "full_doc_id": "doc-568be3f381f63ce6af340efc5aa8c5bb", "file_path": "unknown_source"}, {"__id__": "chunk-affbd5dbcf34ebe169fa07a29874fc52", "__created_at__": 1756582225, "content": "8, Art. 46(2)(c)\", \"snippet\": \"‘Pseudonymisation’ definition [Art.4(5)]. Personal data must be adequate, relevant and limited (data minimisation) [Art.5(1)(c)] and kept no longer than necessary (storage limitation) [Art.5(1)(e)]. Processing lawful if subject has given consent [Art.6(1)(a)] or on other lawful bases (e.g., legitimate interests [Art.6(1)(f)]). Special rules for child consent [Art.8]. Standard Contractual Clauses and safeguards required for transfers to third countries [Art.46].\", \"source_id\": \"[DC] GDPR_EU_GENERAL\"}, {\"name\": \"Commission Recommendation and preparatory DSA materials (context)\", \"citation\": \"Commission Recommendation (EU) 2018/334; DSA impact/implementation materials\", \"snippet\": \"DSA builds on prior Commission Recommendation regarding illegal content and includes preparatory materials and impact assessments referenced in document chunks.\", \"source_id\": \"[DC] digital_services_act_wiki.txt\"}]}, \"past_case_references\": [{\"case_id\": \"DSA_VLOP_Enforcement_Dec2023\", \"similarity_reason\": \"Document chunks note DSA enforcement activity as of Dec 2023 involving platforms that provide video content, indicating regulators treat video hosting services as in-scope for enforcement.\", \"source_id\": \"[DC] digital_services_act_wiki.txt\"}, {\"case_id\": \"no-context_verdict_1\", \"similarity_reason\": \"Historical verdict string provided in inputs is uninformative and supplies no actionable precedent ('Sorry, I'm not able to provide an answer to that question.[no-context]'). Included for traceability.\", \"source_id\": \"[KG] Sorry, I'm not able to provide an answer to that question.\"}], \"confidence\": 0.75}", "full_doc_id": "doc-568be3f381f63ce6af340efc5aa8c5bb", "file_path": "unknown_source"}, {"__id__": "chunk-a7af9efd9e1ca7900a1c92ce052255e7", "__created_at__": 1756582575, "content": "{\"feature\": \"Trial run of video replies in EU\", \"feature_description\": \"Roll out video reply functionality to users in the EEA only. GH (Geo-handler; a module responsible for routing features based on user region) (Geo-handler) will gate exposure by region; BB (Baseline Behavior; standard user behavior used for anomaly detection) (Baseline Behavior) will collect telemetry/analytics for anomaly detection and feedback.\", \"needs_geo_specific_logic\": true, \"reasoning\": \"Multiple Jury reports agree the feature is region-targeted (EEA per feature text) and combines hosting user-generated video content with telemetry, which triggers EU-specific obligations under the Digital Services Act (DSA) and personal-data obligations under the GDPR. Product-level geo-specific logic is required so GH (Geo-handler; a module responsible for routing features based on user region) can enforce EEA-only exposure and produce auditable logs proving regional gating. Operational implications include implementing DSA notice-and-action intake/triage/takedown/logging and internal complaint-handling and transparency reporting workflows (Articles 14–16, 15/24, 20), documenting recommender-system parameters and user controls if algorithmic surfacing is used (Article 27), and preparing for VLOP-level systemic risk assessments/audits if designation thresholds apply (Articles 33–37). BB (Baseline Behavior; standard user behavior used for anomaly detection) telemetry must be designed to comply with GDPR principles: data minimisation and storage limitation (Art.5(1)(c) & 5(1)(e)), pseudonymisation (Art.4(5)), lawful-basis selection and recording (Art.6(1)(a)/(f)), child-consent protections (Art.8), DPIA when high risk (Art.35), and cross-border transfer safeguards (Art.46(2)(c) / SCCs). Conflicts / open items: the feature text explicitly states EEA, but some metadata references 'EU' — this territorial-scope discrepancy should be resolved before rollout (GH (Geo-handler; a module responsible for routing features based on user region) config must reflect the confirmed scope). Also, the available document chunks are secondary summaries (wiki and guidance); legal should validate exact obligations and reporting formats against authoritative EUR-Lex/GDPR texts before implementation.\", \"regions_affected\": {\"region\": \"EEA (feature text: EEA; confirm whether authoritative scope is EEA or EU before rollout)\", \"requirement_summary\": \"Enforce EEA-only exposure via GH (Geo-handler; a module responsible for routing features based on user region) (with auditable geo-logs); implement DSA-compliant content workflows (notice-and-action intake, takedown, triage, logging, transparency reporting, internal complaint handling); document and expose recommender-system parameters / user controls if replies are surfaced by algorithms; design BB (Baseline Behavior; standard user behavior used for anomaly detection) telemetry to minimise personal data, apply pseudonymisation, limit retention, select and record lawful bases, implement age/consent flows for children, and apply cross-border transfer safeguards (SCCs/Art.46) if processing/storage is outside the EEA; prepare for VLOP-level obligations (systemic risk assessments, mitigation, audits) if designated under Articles 33–37.\", \"regulations\": [{\"name\": \"Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)\", \"citation\": \"Articles 14–16\", \"snippet\": \"Obligations for providers of hosting services to operate notice-and-action mechanisms allowing recipients or other entities to notify the provider of allegedly illegal content and to take appropriate and expeditious action.\", \"source_id\": \"[DC] digital_services_act_wiki.txt\"}, {\"name\": \"Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)\", \"citation\": \"Article 15 / Article 24\", \"snippet\": \"Transparency reporting obligations for providers of intermediary services (publishable and machine-readable reporting on content-moderation decisions, complaints handling, and the use of automated tools).\", \"source_id\": \"EUR-Lex 32022R2065\"}, {\"name\": \"Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)\", \"citation\": \"Article 20\", \"snippet\": \"Requirement to maintain an internal complaint-handling system for users to challenge content moderation decisions and receive reasoned responses.\", \"source_id\": \"EUR-Lex 32022R2065\"}, {\"name\": \"Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)\", \"citation\": \"Article 27\", \"snippet\": \"Recommender-system transparency: platforms using recommender systems must disclose in plain language the main parameters used and provide options for recipients to modify or influence those parameters.\", \"source_id\": \"https://www.eu-digital-services-act.com/Digital_Services_Act_Article_27.html\"}, {\"name\": \"Digital Services Act (Regulation (EU) 2022/2065)\", \"citation\": \"Articles 33–37\", \"snippet\": \"Designation thresholds and VLOP-specific obligations, including systemic risk assessments, mitigation measures and independent audits/reporting for designated very large online platforms.\", \"source_id\": \"[DC] digital_services_act_wiki.txt\"}, {\"name\": \"General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR)\", \"citation\": \"Art.4(5), Art.5(1)(c), Art.5(1)(e), Art.6(1)(a)/(f), Art.8, Art.35, Art.46(2)(c)\", \"snippet\": \"Definitions and principles relevant to telemetry and personal data: pseudonymisation definition [Art.4(5)]; data minimisation [Art.5(1)(c)]; storage limitation [Art.5(1)(e)];", "full_doc_id": "doc-a118e1022441be66aca33cadb2730800", "file_path": "unknown_source"}, {"__id__": "chunk-056ac303c97fb8156cbad15ed2fbe410", "__created_at__": 1756582575, "content": "\"Art.4(5), Art.5(1)(c), Art.5(1)(e), Art.6(1)(a)/(f), Art.8, Art.35, Art.46(2)(c)\", \"snippet\": \"Definitions and principles relevant to telemetry and personal data: pseudonymisation definition [Art.4(5)]; data minimisation [Art.5(1)(c)]; storage limitation [Art.5(1)(e)]; lawful bases (consent [Art.6(1)(a)] vs legitimate interests [Art.6(1)(f)]); child-consent rules [Art.8]; DPIA for high-risk processing [Art.35]; safeguards for international transfers / SCCs [Art.46(2)(c)].\", \"source_id\": \"[DC] GDPR_EU_GENERAL\"}, {\"name\": \"Commission Recommendation / DSA preparatory materials (context)\", \"citation\": \"Commission Recommendation (EU) 2018/334; DSA impact/implementation materials\", \"snippet\": \"Context and preparatory material used in shaping DSA obligations (useful for implementation guidance and enforcement context).\", \"source_id\": \"[DC] digital_services_act_wiki.txt\"}, {\"name\": \"GDPR — Art.5 guidance (data minimisation)\", \"citation\": \"Art.5(1)(c)\", \"snippet\": \"Principle that personal data must be adequate, relevant and limited to what is necessary for the purposes of processing (data minimisation) — practical guidance referenced.\", \"source_id\": \"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-5-gdpr/\"}]}, \"past_case_references\": [{\"case_id\": \"DSA_VLOP_Enforcement_Dec2023\", \"similarity_reason\": \"DSA enforcement activity noted in Dec 2023 involving platforms providing video content; supports that video-hosting features are in-scope for regulator scrutiny under the DSA.\", \"source_id\": \"[DC] digital_services_act_wiki.txt\"}, {\"case_id\": \"no-context_verdict_1\", \"similarity_reason\": \"Provided input string is uninformative but included for traceability to the KG entry.\", \"source_id\": \"[KG] Sorry, I'm not able to provide an answer to that question.\"}], \"confidence\": 0.8}", "full_doc_id": "doc-a118e1022441be66aca33cadb2730800", "file_path": "unknown_source"}], "matrix": ""}